.\"
.\" SEM_OPEN(3) - Substrate Pthreads Library
.\"
.TH SEM_OPEN 3 "June 2026" "Substrate OS" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
sem_open \- open (create or attach to) a named semaphore
.SH LIBRARY
POSIX threads library
.RI ( libpthread ", " \-lpthread )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fcntl.h>
.B #include <sys/stat.h>
.B #include <semaphore.h>
.PP
.BI "sem_t *sem_open(const char *" name ", int " oflag ");"
.BI "sem_t *sem_open(const char *" name ", int " oflag ,
.BI "                mode_t " mode ", unsigned int " value ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR sem_open ()
creates a new POSIX named semaphore or opens an existing one, returning a
.I sem_t
pointer usable with
.BR sem_wait (3),
.BR sem_post (3),
.BR sem_getvalue (3),
and closed with
.BR sem_close (3).
.PP
On Substrate a named semaphore is backed by a real kernel object (a
.IR ksem ,
see
.BR ksem_open (2)),
so it synchronises correctly across unrelated processes.  This is different
from an unnamed
.BR sem_init (3)
semaphore with
.I pshared
equal to 0, which is a fast process-local futex word.
.PP
.I name
identifies the semaphore system-wide and must begin with a slash
.RB ( / )
followed by one or more characters, e.g.
.IR /mysem .
.PP
.I oflag
selects the mode of operation:
.TP
.B O_CREAT
Create the semaphore if it does not exist.  When
.B O_CREAT
is set,
.I mode
and
.I value
must be supplied:
.I mode
sets the permission bits (modified by the process umask) and
.I value
is the initial count (0 through
.BR SEM_VALUE_MAX ).
If the semaphore already exists,
.B O_CREAT
without
.B O_EXCL
simply attaches to it and
.I mode
and
.I value
are ignored.
.TP
.B O_EXCL
Together with
.BR O_CREAT ,
require that the semaphore not already exist; fail with
.B EEXIST
otherwise.
.PP
Each successful
.BR sem_open ()
adds one open reference.  The semaphore persists until it is removed with
.BR sem_unlink (3)
.I and
every open descriptor has been closed with
.BR sem_close (3),
matching POSIX persistence semantics.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success
.BR sem_open ()
returns a pointer to the semaphore.  On failure it returns
.B SEM_FAILED
and sets
.I errno
to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EACCES
The semaphore exists but the caller lacks permission to open it.
.TP
.B EEXIST
.B O_CREAT
and
.B O_EXCL
were both set but the semaphore already exists.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I name
does not begin with a slash followed by at least one character, or
.I value
exceeds
.BR SEM_VALUE_MAX .
.TP
.B ENAMETOOLONG
.I name
is too long.
.TP
.B ENOENT
.B O_CREAT
was not set and the named semaphore does not exist.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
Insufficient memory to allocate the semaphore handle.
.TP
.B ENOSPC
The system limit on the number of named semaphores would be exceeded.
.SH EXAMPLE
Two processes rendezvous through a named semaphore:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    sem_t *s = sem_open("/example", O_CREAT | O_RDWR, 0666, 0);
    if (s == SEM_FAILED) { perror("sem_open"); return 1; }

    /* ... a peer process opens "/example" and calls sem_post() ... */
    sem_wait(s);            /* blocks until the peer posts */

    sem_close(s);
    sem_unlink("/example"); /* remove the name once done */
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sem_close (3),
.BR sem_unlink (3),
.BR sem_wait (3),
.BR sem_post (3),
.BR sem_getvalue (3),
.BR sem_init (3),
.BR ksem_open (2)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2017.
